allwinner-i2c: the soft reset clears itself

TWI_SRST bit 0 is a pulse on silicon: writing 1 resets the controller
and the bit reads 0 once that is done ("write 1, cleared to 0" in the
Allwinner register descriptions).  The model held the controller in
reset until 0 was written, so drivers that set the bit and poll for it
to clear -- the sun8iw19 (V831) vendor boot0, U-Boot 2018.05 and Linux
4.9 i2c-sunxi.c -- saw it stay set and every later CNTR write ignored:
each transfer ran into the driver's timeout.  Linux mainline's mv64xxx
writes the register and does not read it, so it is not affected.

---
diff --git a/hw/i2c/allwinner-i2c.c b/hw/i2c/allwinner-i2c.c
index fe887e1..e6c7867 100644
--- a/hw/i2c/allwinner-i2c.c
+++ b/hw/i2c/allwinner-i2c.c
@@ -384,10 +384,15 @@ static void allwinner_i2c_write(void *opaque, hwaddr offset,
         s->ccr = value & TWI_CCR_MASK;
         break;
     case TWI_SRST_REG:
-        if (((value & TWI_SRST_MASK) == 0) && (s->srst & TWI_SRST_MASK)) {
+        /*
+         * The reset is a pulse: writing 1 resets the controller, and the
+         * bit reads back 0 once that is done, which is what the Allwinner
+         * vendor boot0, U-Boot and Linux poll for.
+         */
+        if (value & TWI_SRST_MASK) {
             device_cold_reset(DEVICE(s));
         }
-        s->srst = value & TWI_SRST_MASK;
+        s->srst = TWI_SRST_RESET;
         break;
     case TWI_EFR_REG:
         s->efr = value & TWI_EFR_MASK;
